nodecode-skill-router is a rewrite of DECRUX9812's DECRUX9812/typesafe-skill-router:
https://github.com/DECRUX9812/typesafe-skill-router
commit e6cdac26f9ed588b4a94b8a2f7f9f026e1b9faf3

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Ritesh Patel (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the two-stage TypeSafe (System One) routing decision -- the
chunked choice over the roster with its three request judgments and gate, the
shortlist stage with one fit judgment per candidate, both thresholds, the
`none_of_these` option and the tie-break by name, the one `<skill_relevance>`
line, and the fail-safe posture (nothing sent without a key, nothing said
when nothing fits, every failure silent). The Hermes `pre_llm_call` hook
became Nodecode's `:round` point, which records the line as the reminder of
the round about to be asked; the roster, read there from `~/.hermes/skills`
and their `SKILL.md`, is here what the index lists -- the skills and linked
`SKILL.md` skills this image keeps. The request is the turn's own committed
text, read from the event log. Python's stdlib client became a hand-written
JSON writer and `nlk:http`.

Left out: the on-disk answer cache (`cache.json`), the per-turn log file,
and the `/typesafe-skill-router on|off|status|suggest|check` command family.
A cell's section is its on/off switch and the answers are cheap enough to
ask again, so the cache and the log had nothing left to do; the command is
one `/skill-router` that answers status and routes one request on demand.
The thread-pool budget around a routing decision is gone too: `nlk:http`
takes a timeout of its own.
